Hyper.catalog("aerodynamics", [
  ["aerodynamics","Hyper Aerodynamics","How air flows and what it does to everything that moves through it — from a sheet of paper to an airliner, a racing car, a wind turbine and a swift — explained, calculated and simulated.",""],
  ["air-and-flow-basics","Air and Flow Basics","Air as a fluid — its density, pressure, viscosity and speed of sound — and the dimensionless numbers (Reynolds, Mach, force coefficients) that let a model in a tunnel speak for a full-size aircraft.",""],
  ["air-properties","Air as a fluid","What a fluid is; density, pressure and viscosity of air; the ideal gas; the speed of sound.","Air and Flow Basics"],
  ["what-is-a-fluid","What a fluid is","A fluid is any substance that keeps deforming for as long as a sideways (shear) force acts on it, however small the force. Liquids and gases are both fluids; air is one made of widely spaced, fast-moving molecules, yet s","Air and Flow Basics",["physics:density","physics:stress-strain","physics:kinetic-theory-gases","physics:mean-free-path"]],
  ["air-density","Air density","Air has mass: a cubic metre at sea level weighs about 1.2 kg. Its density ρ rises with pressure, falls with temperature and humidity, and drops with height — and every aerodynamic force is proportional to it.","Air and Flow Basics",["physics:density","physics:ideal-gas-law","physics:buoyancy"]],
  ["air-pressure","Pressure in air","Pressure is the push of the air on a surface per square metre — the combined impacts of countless molecules. At sea level it is about 101 kPa, the weight of the whole column of air above; it falls with height, acts equal","Air and Flow Basics",["physics:pressure","physics:hydrostatic-pressure","physics:kinetic-theory-gases"]],
  ["air-viscosity","Viscosity of air","Viscosity is a fluid's internal friction — its resistance to layers sliding over one another. Air's dynamic viscosity is tiny, 1.8 × 10⁻⁵ Pa·s, fifty-five times less than water's; but its kinematic viscosity μ/ρ is fifte","Air and Flow Basics",["physics:viscosity","physics:mean-free-path","physics:kinetic-theory-gases"]],
  ["ideal-gas-air","Air as an ideal gas","Air obeys the ideal-gas law p = ρRT to better than 0.1 % in almost all aerodynamics, with R = 287 J/(kg·K) and γ = cp/cv = 1.4. Squeeze it quickly and it heats; let it expand and it cools — the key to sound, nozzles, com","Air and Flow Basics",["physics:ideal-gas-law","physics:first-law-thermodynamics","physics:specific-heat","physics:thermodynamic-processes","physics:equipartition"]],
  ["speed-of-sound","The speed of sound","Sound is a small pressure disturbance that travels through air at a = √(γRT): 340 m/s at 15 °C, 295 m/s at airliner cruising height. It depends on temperature, not on pressure — and it is the speed at which the air ahead","Air and Flow Basics",["physics:speed-of-sound","physics:sound-waves","physics:doppler-effect","physics:shock-waves"]],
  ["similarity","Dimensionless numbers","Dimensional analysis, dynamic pressure, force coefficients, and the Reynolds, Mach and Strouhal numbers.","Air and Flow Basics"],
  ["dimensional-analysis","Dimensional analysis","Every physical law must balance in its dimensions — mass, length, time, temperature. That alone can reveal the form of a law, and the Buckingham Π theorem shrinks a problem with n quantities to n − k dimensionless groups","Air and Flow Basics",["math:exponents","math:systems-of-equations","physics:newtons-second-law","math:scaling-laws"]],
  ["dynamic-pressure","Dynamic pressure","Dynamic pressure q = ½ρV² is the kinetic energy of a cubic metre of moving air — and the rise in pressure when that air is brought to rest. Every aerodynamic force and pressure scales with it, F = C·q·S, and it grows wit","Air and Flow Basics",["physics:kinetic-energy","physics:bernoullis-equation"]],
  ["force-coefficients","Force and moment coefficients","A force coefficient is an aerodynamic force divided by the dynamic pressure and a reference area, C = F/(qS). Lift, drag, side-force, moment and pressure coefficients describe a shape by pure numbers that hardly change w","Air and Flow Basics",["physics:torque"]],
  ["reynolds-number","The Reynolds number","The Reynolds number Re = ρVL/μ = VL/ν compares the inertia of a flow with its viscosity. It decides the character of every flow — creeping, laminar or turbulent — and runs from 10⁻⁵ for a swimming bacterium to 10⁹ for a ","Air and Flow Basics"],
  ["mach-number","The Mach number","The Mach number M = V/a compares a speed with the local speed of sound. It measures how much the air is compressed: below M ≈ 0.3 hardly at all, near M = 1 the flow is transonic with shock waves, and above it the air ahe","Air and Flow Basics"],
  ["strouhal-froude","Strouhal, Froude and other numbers","Beyond Reynolds and Mach: the Strouhal number fL/V measures unsteadiness (vortex shedding, flapping wings), the Froude number V/√(gL) the balance of inertia and gravity (ship waves, towing tanks, walking), the Weber numb","Air and Flow Basics",["physics:wave-properties","physics:surface-tension"]],
  ["atmosphere","The Atmosphere","The International Standard Atmosphere, how pressure, temperature and density fall with height, what altimeters really measure — and the wind, turbulence, shear and ice that real air adds.",""],
  ["standard-atmosphere","The standard atmosphere","ISA, the layers of the atmosphere, the lapse rate, pressure altitude, density altitude and humidity.","The Atmosphere"],
  ["isa","The International Standard Atmosphere","An agreed, average atmosphere — 15 °C and 1013.25 hPa at sea level, cooling by 6.5 °C per kilometre to −56.5 °C at 11 km — against which altimeters are calibrated and every aircraft performance figure is quoted.","The Atmosphere",["physics:hydrostatic-pressure"]],
  ["atmospheric-layers","Layers of the atmosphere","The atmosphere is layered by how its temperature changes with height: the weather-filled troposphere, the calm stratosphere warmed by ozone, the frigid mesosphere and the thin, hot thermosphere — with nearly all the air,","The Atmosphere",["physics:hydrostatic-pressure"]],
  ["lapse-rate","The temperature lapse rate","How fast the temperature falls with height. Comparing the actual (environmental) lapse rate with the rate at which a rising parcel of air cools — 9.8 °C per km when dry, about 5–6 °C per km in cloud — decides whether the","The Atmosphere",["physics:thermodynamic-processes","physics:first-law-thermodynamics","physics:buoyancy"]],
  ["pressure-altitude","Pressure altitude and the altimeter","An altimeter is a barometer with a height scale: it converts the outside static pressure into a height using the standard atmosphere. Set to QNH it shows altitude above sea level, to QFE height above the airfield, and to","The Atmosphere"],
  ["density-altitude","Density altitude","The height in the standard atmosphere at which the air would be as thin as it is now. On a hot day at a high airfield it can be thousands of feet above the field's elevation — and wings, propellers and engines all perfor","The Atmosphere"],
  ["humidity-air","Humidity and air density","Water vapour is lighter than the nitrogen and oxygen it replaces, so humid air is less dense than dry air at the same pressure and temperature — by 1 to 2 % on a hot, muggy day. Humidity also sets the dew point, where cl","The Atmosphere",["physics:ideal-gas-law","physics:latent-heat"]],
  ["weather-flight","Wind and weather","The wind gradient, turbulence and gusts, thermals and mountain waves, wind shear and icing.","The Atmosphere"],
  ["wind-gradient","Wind and the wind gradient","Friction with the ground slows the wind near the surface, so the wind grows with height through the lowest few hundred metres — roughly logarithmically. It matters for aircraft and gliders on the approach, for wind turbi","The Atmosphere",["math:logarithms"]],
  ["atmospheric-turbulence","Atmospheric turbulence and gusts","Irregular air motion — over rough ground, in thermals, where the wind shears near the jet stream, under mountain waves and in the wakes of other aircraft — that jolts an aircraft by changing its angle of attack. How hard","The Atmosphere"],
  ["thermals-waves","Thermals and mountain waves","Two ways the atmosphere lifts a glider for free: thermals — bubbles and columns of sun-warmed air rising through unstable air — and mountain waves, standing oscillations set up when stable air flows over a ridge, which c","The Atmosphere",["physics:buoyancy","physics:convection","physics:simple-harmonic-motion"]],
  ["wind-shear","Wind shear and microbursts","A change of wind speed or direction over a short distance. Near the ground — in a microburst's outflow, at a front or under a low-level jet — it can take airspeed and height from an aircraft faster than its engines can g","The Atmosphere"],
  ["icing","Icing","Supercooled water droplets — liquid below 0 °C — freeze on the parts of an aircraft they hit. A few millimetres of rough ice on a wing's leading edge can cut the maximum lift by a third, raise the stall speed and the dra","The Atmosphere"],
  ["flow-physics","How Air Flows","Streamlines, conservation of mass and momentum, Bernoulli's equation, vorticity and circulation, how airspeed is measured, and the elegant world of potential flow.",""],
  ["flow-description","Describing flow","Streamlines, continuity, Bernoulli, the momentum equation, Navier–Stokes, vorticity and circulation.","How Air Flows"],
  ["streamlines","Streamlines, pathlines and streaklines","Three ways to draw a flow: a streamline follows the velocity at one instant, a pathline follows one particle over time, and a streakline joins every particle that has passed one point — the line smoke shows. In steady fl","How Air Flows",["math:vectors"]],
  ["continuity","Conservation of mass","Air is neither made nor destroyed: whatever mass flows into a region must flow out or pile up inside. In a steady duct flow the mass flow ṁ = ρAV is the same at every section — so a narrowing duct speeds the air up.","How Air Flows",["physics:continuity-equation"]],
  ["bernoulli","Bernoulli's equation","Along a streamline in steady, frictionless, incompressible flow, p + ½ρV² + ρgz stays constant: where air speeds up its pressure falls. It is energy conservation — Newton's second law — for a moving fluid, and it holds o","How Air Flows",["physics:bernoullis-equation","physics:work-energy"]],
  ["momentum-equation","The momentum equation","Newton's second law for a flowing fluid: the net force on the fluid inside a control volume equals the momentum per second flowing out minus the momentum per second flowing in. It gives the thrust of engines, the force o","How Air Flows",["physics:newtons-second-law","physics:momentum","physics:newtons-third-law"]],
  ["navier-stokes","The Navier–Stokes equations","The equations of motion of a viscous fluid: Newton's second law for every small parcel of air, with pressure, viscous stress and gravity as the forces. With continuity they govern every flow in this app — and in general ","How Air Flows",["math:partial-derivatives","math:vector-calculus"]],
  ["vorticity-circulation","Vorticity and circulation","Vorticity measures how fast a small blob of air is spinning (twice its rate of rotation); circulation is the total swirl round a closed loop, ∮V·ds. Stokes' theorem links them, Kelvin's theorem conserves circulation, and","How Air Flows",["math:curl","math:line-integrals","math:stokes-theorem"]],
  ["measuring-airspeed","Measuring airspeed","Stagnation and total pressure, the pitot-static system and the four airspeeds.","How Air Flows"],
  ["stagnation-point","Stagnation and total pressure","Where the air meets a body head-on it is brought to rest. At this stagnation point the pressure is the highest anywhere on the body: the total pressure, p₀ = p + ½ρV² at low speed. At high speed the air is also compresse","How Air Flows"],
  ["pitot-tube","The pitot-static tube","A tube facing into the airflow measures total pressure; holes on the side measure static pressure; the difference is the impact pressure, which gives the airspeed. Nearly every aircraft measures its speed this way — and ","How Air Flows"],
  ["airspeeds","IAS, CAS, EAS and TAS","An aircraft has four airspeeds. IAS is what the dial shows; CAS removes instrument and position error; EAS removes the compressibility of the air at that height; TAS — the real speed through the air — is EAS scaled by √(","How Air Flows"],
  ["potential-flow","Potential flow","Superposition of simple flows, the cylinder, Kutta–Joukowski, the Magnus effect and d'Alembert's paradox.","How Air Flows"],
  ["potential-flow-basics","Potential flow and superposition","Treat air as frictionless, incompressible and free of spin, and its whole velocity field follows from a single function that obeys Laplace's equation. Because that equation is linear, simple flows can be added together t","How Air Flows",["math:laplace-equation","math:gradient","math:complex-numbers"]],
  ["sources-vortices","Sources, sinks, doublets and vortices","The building blocks of potential flow: a source spreading air outward, a sink swallowing it, a vortex swirling it round, and a doublet — a source and sink pressed together — which, added to a stream, draws a circular cyl","How Air Flows",["physics:angular-momentum"]],
  ["cylinder-flow","Flow around a cylinder","The classic potential flow: a uniform stream plus a doublet wraps the air round a circular cylinder. The air doubles its speed at the top and bottom, the pressure pattern is perfectly symmetric — and adding a vortex move","How Air Flows",["math:complex-numbers"]],
  ["kutta-joukowski","The Kutta–Joukowski theorem","The lift per metre of span of any two-dimensional body in a steady, frictionless stream is the air density times the stream speed times the circulation round it: L′ = ρV∞Γ. The shape matters only through the circulation ","How Air Flows",["math:line-integrals"]],
  ["magnus-effect","The Magnus effect","A spinning ball or cylinder moving through air is pushed sideways, towards the side where its surface moves along with the air streaming past — the bend of a free kick, the dip of a topspin forehand, the long carry of a ","How Air Flows",["physics:projectile-motion","physics:drag-force"]],
  ["dalembert-paradox","D'Alembert's paradox","In a perfectly frictionless fluid a body moving at steady speed feels no drag at all, whatever its shape — a result d'Alembert reached in 1752 that flatly contradicts experience. The way out is the thin boundary layer, w","How Air Flows"],
  ["viscous-flow","Viscosity and Boundary Layers","The no-slip condition and the thin boundary layer where all friction lives: laminar and turbulent layers, transition, skin friction, separation, the drag crisis, vortex shedding and turbulence.",""],
  ["boundary-layers","Boundary layers","No slip, laminar and turbulent layers, transition and skin friction.","Viscosity and Boundary Layers"],
  ["no-slip","The no-slip condition","Air touching a solid surface moves with the surface — relative to the wall it is at rest. This single fact creates the boundary layer, skin friction, separation and, in the end, both drag and lift.","Viscosity and Boundary Layers",["physics:viscosity","physics:mean-free-path"]],
  ["boundary-layer","The boundary layer","The thin layer next to a surface where the air speed climbs from zero to the full stream and all the friction lives. Prandtl's 1904 idea of splitting a flow into this viscous layer and a frictionless outer stream made ae","Viscosity and Boundary Layers"],
  ["laminar-boundary-layer","The laminar boundary layer","A smooth, orderly boundary layer whose profile Blasius solved exactly in 1908: thickness 5x/√Re_x, displacement thickness 1.72x/√Re_x, momentum thickness 0.664x/√Re_x and very low skin friction — but little resistance to","Viscosity and Boundary Layers",["math:differential-equations"]],
  ["turbulent-boundary-layer","The turbulent boundary layer","A churning boundary layer full of eddies: thicker than a laminar one, with a full, blunt velocity profile, three to eight times the skin friction — and far more resistance to separation. Near the wall it follows the univ","Viscosity and Boundary Layers"],
  ["transition","Transition to turbulence","Where and how a smooth laminar boundary layer turns turbulent — on a flat plate typically near Re_x ≈ 5 × 10⁵, but anywhere from 10⁵ to 3 × 10⁶ depending on free-stream turbulence, pressure gradient, roughness and noise.","Viscosity and Boundary Layers"],
  ["skin-friction","Skin friction","The drag of air rubbing along a surface: the wall shear stress added up over the wetted area, D_f = C_f · ½ρU² · S_wet. Laminar layers have little, turbulent ones several times more, and on an airliner it is about half t","Viscosity and Boundary Layers"],
  ["separation-wakes","Separation and wakes","Adverse pressure gradients and separation, the drag crisis, vortex shedding, flow control and turbulence.","Viscosity and Boundary Layers"],
  ["flow-separation","Flow separation","When the pressure rises along a surface, the slow air near the wall can run out of energy, stop and turn back; the boundary layer then lifts off the surface into a turbulent wake. Separation causes pressure drag, stall, ","Viscosity and Boundary Layers"],
  ["drag-crisis","The drag crisis","Near a Reynolds number of 3 × 10⁵ the drag coefficient of a smooth sphere or cylinder suddenly drops by a factor of three to five: the boundary layer turns turbulent before it separates, clings on further round the back ","Viscosity and Boundary Layers"],
  ["vortex-shedding","Vortex shedding and wakes","Behind a cylinder or any bluff body the separated shear layers roll up and shed vortices alternately from each side — a Kármán vortex street — at a frequency f = St·V/d with a Strouhal number of about 0.2. It makes wires","Viscosity and Boundary Layers",["physics:driven-oscillations"]],
  ["flow-control","Flow control: vortex generators and more","Ways to make the boundary layer do what you want: vortex generators and turbulators that re-energise it against separation, suction and blowing that remove or feed it, and riblets and laminar-flow control that cut its fr","Viscosity and Boundary Layers"],
  ["turbulence","Turbulence","The chaotic, swirling state of most real flows: eddies of every size, from the scale of the flow down to the Kolmogorov scale, pass energy down a cascade until viscosity turns it into heat. It mixes momentum, heat and po","Viscosity and Boundary Layers",["physics:convection"]],
  ["airfoils","Airfoils","The wing section: its shape, how it turns the flow to make lift, the lift equation and the lift curve, pressure distributions, pitching moment, stall, thin-airfoil theory and the flaps and slats of landing.",""],
  ["airfoil-basics","Airfoil shape and lift","Airfoil geometry, how lift works, the lift equation, the pressure coefficient and distribution, the Kutta condition, NACA sections.","Airfoils"],
  ["airfoil-geometry","Airfoil geometry","The anatomy of a wing section: a rounded leading edge, a sharp trailing edge, the chord line between them, a mean camber line that sets how curved it is, a thickness wrapped round that line — and the angle of attack betw","Airfoils"],
  ["how-lift-works","How lift works","A wing lifts by turning the passing air downwards. To curve the air's path it must set up a pressure field — low above, a little higher below — and that pressure field is the lift. Newton, Bernoulli and circulation are t","Airfoils",["physics:newtons-third-law","physics:bernoullis-equation"]],
  ["pressure-coefficient","The pressure coefficient","The pressure coefficient C_p compares the pressure at a point with the free-stream pressure, in units of the dynamic pressure: 1 at a stagnation point, 0 where the air moves at free-stream speed, negative where it runs f","Airfoils",["physics:bernoullis-equation"]],
  ["pressure-distribution","Pressure distribution on an airfoil","The plot of pressure coefficient along the upper and lower surfaces is an airfoil's signature: a stagnation point at the nose, a suction peak just behind it, a long climb back towards the trailing edge. The area between ","Airfoils"],
  ["kutta-condition","The Kutta condition","Frictionless flow round an airfoil has a solution for every value of circulation, and so for every value of lift. The Kutta condition picks the one real flow: the air must leave the sharp trailing edge smoothly. Nature e","Airfoils"],
  ["naca-airfoils","NACA airfoils","The NACA airfoil families of the 1930s–40s turned wing sections into a catalogue: a NACA 2412 has 2 % camber at 40 % of the chord and is 12 % thick. Their simple formulas, published test data and clear naming made them t","Airfoils"],
  ["lift-equation","The lift equation","Lift equals the dynamic pressure of the oncoming air, times the wing area, times a lift coefficient that carries everything about the wing's shape and angle: L = ½ρV²SC_L. It explains why aircraft need runways, why they ","Airfoils",["physics:bernoullis-equation"]],
  ["airfoil-behaviour","Airfoil behaviour","The lift curve, stall, pitching moment, centre of pressure, thin-airfoil theory, Reynolds effects, high-lift devices and special sections.","Airfoils"],
  ["lift-curve","The lift curve","Plot lift coefficient against angle of attack and you get an airfoil's lift curve: a straight line of slope about 0.1 per degree, crossing zero at the zero-lift angle (negative for cambered sections), bending over near 1","Airfoils"],
  ["stall","Stall","A wing stalls when the angle of attack is so large that the air can no longer follow the upper surface: the boundary layer separates, lift stops growing and then falls, drag soars. It happens at an angle, not at a speed ","Airfoils"],
  ["pitching-moment","Pitching moment and aerodynamic centre","The pressures on an airfoil twist it as well as lift it. About one special point near the quarter chord — the aerodynamic centre — that twisting moment does not change with angle of attack; it depends only on camber, and","Airfoils",["physics:torque"]],
  ["center-of-pressure","Centre of pressure","The centre of pressure is the point where the whole aerodynamic force can be said to act, with no moment. On a cambered airfoil it wanders: forward towards the quarter chord as the angle grows, backward — even off the ai","Airfoils"],
  ["thin-airfoil-theory","Thin-airfoil theory","Replace a thin airfoil by a sheet of vortices along its mean camber line, make the flow follow that line and leave the trailing edge smoothly, and the lift and moment of any thin section follow in closed form: a lift slo","Airfoils",["math:fourier-series"]],
  ["reynolds-effects-airfoil","Reynolds number effects","The same airfoil behaves differently at different Reynolds numbers. The lift slope barely changes, but the maximum lift, the drag and the kind of stall depend on whether the boundary layer is laminar or turbulent — which","Airfoils"],
  ["high-lift-devices","Flaps and slats","Flaps and slats let a wing built for fast cruise also fly slowly for take-off and landing. Trailing-edge flaps add camber (and, if they slide back, area), shifting the lift curve upwards; leading-edge slats let the wing ","Airfoils"],
  ["special-airfoils","Laminar, supercritical and low-Reynolds airfoils","Modern airfoils are designed around their pressure distribution for a job: laminar sections keep the boundary layer laminar for low drag, supercritical sections let the air go supersonic gently over the top for fast airl","Airfoils"],
  ["wings","Wings","Real wings end: planform, aspect ratio, sweep, taper and twist; the tip vortices, downwash and induced drag they cause; lifting-line theory, the elliptic ideal, winglets, ground effect, stall patterns and delta wings.",""],
  ["wing-geometry","Wing geometry","Planform, aspect ratio, sweep, taper and twist.","Wings"],
  ["wing-planform","Wing planform","The shape of a wing seen from above: its span, chords, area, taper and sweep. The planform decides how a wing spreads its lift along the span — and so its induced drag, where it stalls first and how fast it can fly.","Wings"],
  ["aspect-ratio","Aspect ratio","Aspect ratio, AR = b²/S, measures how long and slender a wing is. It is the most important single number for induced drag: sailplanes use 20–50, airliners 8–11, light aircraft about 7, fighters 2–4.","Wings"],
  ["sweep","Wing sweep","Sweeping a wing back (or forward) lets it fly closer to the speed of sound, because its sections feel mainly the part of the airspeed square to the leading edge. The price: less lift per degree, a lower maximum lift, tip","Wings"],
  ["taper-twist","Taper and twist","Taper narrows a wing towards its tips; twist changes the angle of its sections along the span, usually nose-down at the tips (washout). Together they shape the lift distribution, lighten the structure and make the wing s","Wings"],
  ["finite-wing","The finite wing","Tip vortices and wake turbulence, downwash, induced drag, lifting-line theory, the elliptic wing, Oswald efficiency, winglets, ground effect, stall patterns and delta wings.","Wings"],
  ["wingtip-vortices","Wingtip vortices and wake turbulence","Where a wing ends, air spills round the tip from the high pressure below to the low pressure above, and the wake rolls up into two counter-rotating vortices. They carry the wing's downwash, cause its induced drag, and ca","Wings"],
  ["downwash","Downwash","The air behind a lifting wing moves downward — the downwash. It tilts the flow the wing meets by the induced angle, so every section works at a smaller effective angle of attack; it carries the momentum that holds the ai","Wings",["physics:newtons-third-law","physics:momentum"]],
  ["induced-drag","Induced drag","Induced drag is the price of lift on a finite wing: the lift, tilted back by the downwash, has a component against the motion. It grows with the square of the lift and falls with the square of the span and of the speed, ","Wings"],
  ["lifting-line","Prandtl's lifting-line theory","Prandtl's lifting-line theory models a straight wing as one bound vortex of varying strength along the span, shedding a sheet of trailing vortices. From the planform and twist alone it predicts the spanwise lift distribu","Wings",["math:fourier-series","math:systems-of-equations"]],
  ["elliptic-lift","The elliptic lift distribution","Lift that falls from the centre to the tips like half an ellipse gives the same downwash all along the span — and the least induced drag possible for a given lift and span. An untwisted elliptical wing produces it; a tap","Wings",["math:ellipse","math:optimization","math:lagrange-multipliers"]],
  ["oswald-efficiency","The Oswald efficiency factor","The Oswald efficiency factor e folds everything that makes a real aircraft's drag-due-to-lift worse than the elliptic ideal into one number: C_D = C_D,0 + C_L²/(πAR e). Whole aircraft typically have e = 0.7–0.85.","Wings"],
  ["winglets","Winglets","Winglets and other wingtip devices reshape the flow at the tips so that a wing of limited span leaves a less costly wake, cutting induced drag and saving a few per cent of fuel on long flights. They do not abolish the ti","Wings"],
  ["ground-effect","Ground effect","Close to the ground — within about one span — a wing's downwash is blocked and partly cancelled, as if a mirror-image wing lifted downward below the surface. Induced drag falls (by half at a tenth of the span), the lift ","Wings"],
  ["stall-patterns","Stall patterns and washout","A wing does not stall all at once: separation begins where the local lift coefficient first reaches the section maximum, and spreads from there. Rectangular wings stall at the root; tapered and swept-back wings towards t","Wings"],
  ["delta-wings","Delta wings and vortex lift","A delta wing is a triangle in planform: very low aspect ratio, a long root chord and a highly swept leading edge. At high angles its sharp leading edges shed two strong vortices over the top, whose suction adds vortex li","Wings"],
  ["drag","Drag","The drag equation and where drag comes from — skin friction, pressure drag, interference, induced and wave drag — the drag polar and L/D; and drag in daily life: cars, trucks, bikes, balls and falling bodies.",""],
  ["drag-types","Kinds of drag","The drag equation, parasite drag, pressure drag, wave drag, the drag polar and lift-to-drag ratio.","Drag"],
  ["drag-equation","The drag equation","Drag equals the dynamic pressure of the oncoming air, times a reference area, times a drag coefficient that carries the shape: D = ½ρV²C_DA. Double the speed and the drag quadruples; the power to push through the air gro","Drag",["physics:drag-force"]],
  ["parasite-drag","Parasite drag","All the drag an aircraft would have even if its wings made no lift: skin friction, pressure drag and interference drag, plus cooling, leakage and small protrusions. It grows with the square of speed and is summed up in o","Drag"],
  ["form-drag","Pressure (form) drag","The drag that comes from the air pressing harder on the front of a body than on its back. It is small when the boundary layer follows the surface to a fine tail, and large when the flow separates and leaves a wide, low-p","Drag"],
  ["wave-drag","Wave drag","The extra drag of flying near and above the speed of sound, when the air cannot move aside smoothly and shock waves form. It rises steeply past the drag-divergence Mach number, peaks just above Mach 1, and is fought with","Drag",["physics:shock-waves"]],
  ["drag-polar","The drag polar","The curve of an aircraft's drag coefficient against its lift coefficient. For most aircraft it is close to a parabola, C_D = C_D,0 + C_L²/(πARe): a fixed parasite part plus an induced part that grows with the square of t","Drag"],
  ["lift-to-drag","Lift-to-drag ratio","How much lift a wing or an aircraft makes for each newton of drag. In a glide it is the distance travelled forward per metre of height lost; in powered flight it sets the thrust needed — weight divided by L/D — and, thro","Drag"],
  ["drag-everyday","Drag in everyday life","Streamlining, bluff bodies, terminal velocity, cars and trucks, racing downforce, sports balls and cycling.","Drag"],
  ["streamlining","Streamlining","Shaping a body so that the air closes smoothly behind it: a rounded nose, a long gently tapering tail and no sharp steps. A good streamlined shape has a tenth or less of the drag of a blunt body of the same frontal area.","Drag"],
  ["bluff-bodies","Bluff bodies","Bodies the air cannot follow round — plates, cylinders, cubes, trucks, people, buildings. The flow separates, leaves a broad wake and sheds vortices; the drag is almost all pressure drag, C_D is of order one, and for sha","Drag"],
  ["terminal-velocity","Terminal velocity","The steady speed a falling body settles at when air drag has grown to equal its weight: v_t = √(2mg/(ρC_DA)). A skydiver falls at about 200 km/h, a raindrop at 2 to 9 m/s, a cloud droplet at about a centimetre a second.","Drag",["physics:free-fall","physics:newtons-second-law","math:first-order-odes","physics:drag-force","math:euler-method"]],
  ["vehicle-aerodynamics","Road vehicle aerodynamics","Cars, vans, buses and trucks push against air drag, which grows with the square of speed, and rolling resistance, which does not. Above about 65 km/h for a car and 90 km/h for a loaded truck the air takes the larger shar","Drag",["physics:power","physics:friction","physics:work-energy"]],
  ["racing-downforce","Downforce in motorsport","Racing cars use upside-down wings and a shaped floor to make the air press them onto the road. The extra load lets the tyres grip harder, so fast corners can be taken much faster — at the price of drag, and sometimes of ","Drag",["physics:friction","physics:centripetal-force","physics:uniform-circular-motion"]],
  ["sports-aerodynamics","Aerodynamics of sports balls","Balls fly at Reynolds numbers where the boundary layer is on the edge of turning turbulent. Dimples, seams, fuzz and spin decide where the flow separates — giving the long carry of a golf drive, the curve of a free kick,","Drag",["physics:projectile-motion"]],
  ["cycling-aero","Cycling and drafting","On a flat road above about 15 km/h most of a cyclist's effort goes into pushing air. The power grows with the cube of speed, so position, clothing and above all riding in another rider's slipstream — drafting — decide ra","Drag",["physics:power","physics:work-energy"]],
  ["compressible","High-Speed Flight","When air compresses: isentropic flow and stagnation conditions, nozzles and choking, normal and oblique shocks, expansion fans, the Mach cone and sonic boom, transonic flight, the area rule and hypersonic heating.",""],
  ["compressible-basics","Compressible flow","Compressibility, isentropic flow, stagnation properties, speed regimes, nozzles, choking and the de Laval nozzle.","High-Speed Flight"],
  ["compressibility","When air compresses","Every gas can be squeezed, but a flow only notices when its speed is a sizeable fraction of the speed of sound. Below about Mach 0.3 air behaves as if it were incompressible; faster than that its density changes with spe","High-Speed Flight",["physics:speed-of-sound"]],
  ["isentropic-flow","Isentropic flow","Flow with no friction and no heat transfer: the entropy of every parcel stays constant, and pressure, density and temperature are tied together by simple power laws. It describes nozzles, intakes and the flow outside bou","High-Speed Flight",["physics:first-law-thermodynamics","physics:entropy","physics:thermodynamic-processes"]],
  ["stagnation-properties","Stagnation temperature and pressure","Bring a moving gas to rest without losses and it reaches its stagnation (total) temperature and pressure. They are what a thermometer on the nose or a pitot tube actually senses, and they explain why fast aircraft get ho","High-Speed Flight",["physics:first-law-thermodynamics"]],
  ["mach-regimes","Subsonic, transonic, supersonic, hypersonic","Aerodynamicists sort flight by Mach number because the physics changes character: effectively incompressible below 0.3, compressible but smooth up to about 0.8, mixed and troublesome near 1, shaped by shock waves above i","High-Speed Flight"],
  ["nozzles","Nozzles and choked flow","A converging nozzle speeds a gas up until its throat reaches the speed of sound. From then on the flow is choked: lowering the downstream pressure no longer increases the mass flow, which depends only on the upstream sta","High-Speed Flight",["physics:ideal-gas-law"]],
  ["de-laval-nozzle","The de Laval nozzle","A nozzle that narrows to a sonic throat and then widens lets a gas accelerate beyond the speed of sound. Rocket engines, supersonic wind tunnels and steam turbines use it. What happens inside — smooth supersonic flow, a ","High-Speed Flight",["physics:rocket-propulsion"]],
  ["shocks","Shocks and expansions","Normal and oblique shocks, Prandtl–Meyer expansion, the Mach cone and the sonic boom.","High-Speed Flight"],
  ["normal-shock","Normal shock waves","A shock wave standing square to the flow. Within a fraction of a micrometre a supersonic stream drops to subsonic speed while its pressure, density and temperature jump. Mass, momentum and energy are conserved across it,","High-Speed Flight",["physics:shock-waves","physics:entropy"]],
  ["oblique-shock","Oblique shock waves","A shock inclined to the flow, formed where a supersonic stream is turned into itself by a wedge, a ramp or a cone. Only the velocity component across the shock is slowed, so the flow is deflected and usually stays supers","High-Speed Flight",["math:vector-components"]],
  ["expansion-fans","Prandtl–Meyer expansion","When a supersonic stream turns away from itself round a convex corner, it expands smoothly through a fan of Mach waves centred on the corner: it speeds up, and its pressure and temperature fall, with no loss. The Prandtl","High-Speed Flight"],
  ["mach-cone","The Mach cone","A body moving faster than sound leaves its sound waves behind inside a cone whose half-angle, the Mach angle μ, has sin μ = 1/M. Outside the cone the air has not yet heard it; its surface is where all the waves pile up.","High-Speed Flight",["physics:doppler-effect","physics:shock-waves","physics:huygens-principle"]],
  ["sonic-boom","The sonic boom","The Mach cone of a supersonic aircraft reaching the ground as two sudden pressure jumps a few tenths of a second apart — an N-shaped wave heard as a double bang. It weakens with height and grows with the aircraft's size ","High-Speed Flight",["physics:shock-waves","physics:sound-intensity"]],
  ["transonic-supersonic","Flying fast","Critical Mach number, transonic flow and buffet, the area rule, sweep, supersonic airfoils and hypersonic heating.","High-Speed Flight"],
  ["critical-mach","Critical Mach number","The flight Mach number at which the air, speeding over the most curved part of a wing, first reaches the speed of sound somewhere on the surface. Below it the flow is subsonic everywhere; above it a pocket of supersonic ","High-Speed Flight"],
  ["transonic-flow","Transonic flow and buffet","From about Mach 0.8 to 1.2 subsonic and supersonic flow share the same aircraft: a supersonic region ends in a shock that grows and moves aft, drag climbs steeply, the boundary layer can separate behind the shock, and th","High-Speed Flight"],
  ["area-rule","The area rule","Near the speed of sound the wave drag of a whole aircraft depends mainly on how its total cross-sectional area grows and shrinks from nose to tail. Smooth that area curve — by waisting the fuselage where the wing joins i","High-Speed Flight"],
  ["swept-wing-compressibility","Sweep and compressibility","Sweeping a wing makes its sections feel only the part of the flow normal to the leading edge, M cos Λ, which delays the critical Mach number and the drag rise. At supersonic speed, enough sweep keeps the leading edge ins","High-Speed Flight"],
  ["supersonic-airfoils","Supersonic airfoils","In supersonic flow an airfoil makes lift and wave drag through shocks and expansion fans. Thin, sharp-edged sections such as the diamond and the biconvex arc work best; Ackeret's linear theory gives c_l = 4α/√(M² − 1) an","High-Speed Flight"],
  ["hypersonic-flight","Hypersonic flight and heating","Above about Mach 5 the air behind the shocks gets so hot that heating, not drag, shapes the vehicle. Stagnation temperatures of thousands of kelvin, thin shock layers and chemically reacting air lead to blunt noses, abla","High-Speed Flight",["physics:thermal-radiation","physics:heat-transfer","physics:convection"]],
  ["performance","Flight Performance","The four forces and steady flight, thrust and power required, best speeds, load factor, turns and the V–n diagram; climb, glide, range and endurance with Breguet, takeoff and landing, ceilings and energy.",""],
  ["forces-in-flight","Forces in flight","The four forces, level flight, thrust and power required, the best speeds, load factor, turns and the V–n diagram.","Flight Performance"],
  ["four-forces","The four forces","Lift, weight, thrust and drag. In steady flight they balance; in a climb lift carries a little less than the weight and the thrust must carry the rest, so a wing lets a small push hold up a large weight.","Flight Performance",["physics:newtons-second-law","physics:free-body-diagrams"]],
  ["level-flight","Steady level flight","In level flight lift equals weight and thrust equals drag. The first condition ties every airspeed to its own lift coefficient and angle of attack; height and weight move the speeds but not the equivalent airspeed.","Flight Performance"],
  ["power-required","Thrust and power required","The drag of an aircraft in level flight is the sum of parasite drag, growing as V², and induced drag, falling as 1/V²: a U-shaped curve. Power required is drag times speed. Where the engine's available thrust or power cr","Flight Performance",["physics:power"]],
  ["minimum-drag-speed","Minimum-drag and minimum-power speeds","Drag is least where induced drag equals parasite drag — the speed of the best lift-to-drag ratio. Power is least at 0.76 of that speed, where induced drag is three times parasite drag. These speeds set the best glide, th","Flight Performance",["math:extrema"]],
  ["load-factor","Load factor","The load factor n is lift divided by weight: the \"g\" felt by everything on board. Turns, pull-ups and gusts raise it; the structure is designed to limit load factors, and the stall speed grows as √n.","Flight Performance",["physics:centripetal-force"]],
  ["turning-flight","Turning flight","An aircraft turns by banking: the tilted lift supplies the centripetal force. In a level turn n = 1/cos φ, the radius is V²/(g tan φ) and the rate g tan φ/V — the same for any aircraft at the same speed and bank. The pri","Flight Performance",["physics:uniform-circular-motion"]],
  ["v-n-diagram","The V–n diagram","The flight envelope on a chart of airspeed against load factor: stall curves on the left, the limit load factors at top and bottom, the dive speed on the right, and the gust lines of rough air. Its corner is the manoeuvr","Flight Performance"],
  ["mission-performance","Climb, glide and range","Climb, gliding, range and endurance, the Breguet equation, takeoff and landing, ceilings and energy management.","Flight Performance"],
  ["climb-performance","Climb performance","An aircraft climbs with whatever the engine gives beyond what level flight needs: the climb angle comes from excess thrust, the rate of climb from excess power. Best angle V_x and best rate V_y, and why both shrink with ","Flight Performance",["physics:gravitational-potential-energy"]],
  ["gliding","Gliding flight","Without thrust an aircraft glides downhill at an angle whose tangent is D/L: the glide ratio equals the lift-to-drag ratio. Best glide at the minimum-drag speed, least sink at the minimum-power speed, and in wind or sink","Flight Performance"],
  ["range-endurance","Range and endurance","Endurance is time aloft per kilogram of fuel, range is distance per kilogram. A jet burns fuel in proportion to thrust, a piston engine in proportion to power, so their best speeds differ: jets stay up longest at V_md an","Flight Performance"],
  ["breguet-range","The Breguet range equation","Range = speed × L/D ÷ fuel consumption × ln(start mass / end mass). Aerodynamics, engine and structure multiply; the logarithm is the price of carrying the fuel you will burn. One form with the overall efficiency covers ","Flight Performance",["math:integration","math:logarithms"]],
  ["takeoff-landing","Takeoff and landing","The takeoff run is an acceleration against drag and wheel friction to the liftoff speed, 1.1–1.2 times the stall speed; its length grows with the square of the weight and falls with density, thrust and headwind. Landing ","Flight Performance",["physics:newtons-second-law"]],
  ["ceiling","Service and absolute ceiling","As an aircraft climbs, the power or thrust available falls while the minimum needed grows; where the rate of climb reaches zero is the absolute ceiling, where it falls to 100 ft/min the service ceiling. Jets meet a secon","Flight Performance"],
  ["energy-management","Energy height and energy management","Height and speed are two stores of the same energy. Energy height h + V²/2g measures their total; the specific excess power (T − D)V/W is how fast it grows. The throttle adds or removes energy, the elevator trades one fo","Flight Performance",["physics:conservation-of-energy","physics:kinetic-energy"]],
  ["stability-control","Stability and Control","Axes and angles, centre of gravity, longitudinal, directional and lateral stability, neutral point and static margin, trim; control surfaces and adverse yaw, the dynamic modes, fly-by-wire and spins.",""],
  ["static-stability","Static stability","Axes and angles, centre of gravity, longitudinal stability, neutral point and static margin, trim, directional and lateral stability.","Stability and Control"],
  ["aircraft-axes","Axes and angles of an aircraft","An aircraft rotates about three body axes — roll about the nose-to-tail axis, pitch about the wing-to-wing axis, yaw about the vertical one. Three Euler angles give its attitude, and the angle of attack α and the sidesli","Stability and Control",["math:vectors","math:coordinate-systems-3d","physics:torque"]],
  ["center-of-gravity","Centre of gravity and balance","The centre of gravity is the point where the aircraft's whole weight acts. Where it sits relative to the wing decides how stable and how controllable the aircraft is, so every flight starts with a weight-and-balance calc","Stability and Control",["physics:center-of-mass","physics:torque"]],
  ["longitudinal-stability","Longitudinal static stability","An aircraft is statically stable in pitch when a nose-up disturbance creates a nose-down moment: its pitching-moment curve slopes downward, C_mα < 0, and crosses zero at a positive angle of attack, where it trims.","Stability and Control",["physics:static-equilibrium"]],
  ["neutral-point","Neutral point and static margin","The neutral point is the CG position at which an aircraft is neutrally stable in pitch. The static margin — how far the CG sits ahead of it, as a fraction of the mean chord — measures how stable the aircraft is.","Stability and Control"],
  ["trim","Trim and the tailplane","To trim is to make the pitching moment zero at the angle of attack — and so the speed — the pilot wants. The elevator or an all-moving tail sets that angle; a trim tab or a movable stabiliser takes away the stick force.","Stability and Control",["physics:torque"]],
  ["directional-stability","Directional stability","Directional, or weathercock, stability is an aircraft's tendency to turn its nose into the relative wind when it sideslips. It comes mostly from the fin and is measured by the yawing-moment slope C_nβ 0.","Stability and Control"],
  ["lateral-stability","Lateral stability and dihedral effect","A banked aircraft slips sideways towards its low wing, and the dihedral effect — from wing dihedral, a high wing or sweepback — turns that sideslip into a rolling moment that raises the low wing: C_lβ < 0.","Stability and Control"],
  ["control-dynamics","Control and dynamics","Control surfaces and adverse yaw, dynamic stability, the phugoid, short period, Dutch roll and spiral, fly-by-wire and spins.","Stability and Control"],
  ["control-surfaces","Control surfaces and adverse yaw","Elevator, ailerons and rudder change the camber of a tail or wing to make a force on a long arm. The ailerons' side effect — extra drag on the rising wing pulling the nose the wrong way — is adverse yaw, tamed by differe","Stability and Control"],
  ["dynamic-stability","Dynamic stability","Static stability says which way an aircraft starts to move after a disturbance; dynamic stability says whether the motion that follows dies away. An aircraft's motion splits into a few natural modes, each with its own pe","Stability and Control",["physics:damped-oscillations","math:damped-oscillator-ode","math:eigenvalues"]],
  ["phugoid","The phugoid","The phugoid is a slow, lightly damped roller-coaster in which an aircraft trades speed for height and back at almost constant angle of attack. Lanchester showed in 1908 that its period is about π√2·V/g — some 0.45 s for ","Stability and Control",["physics:conservation-of-energy","physics:simple-harmonic-motion"]],
  ["short-period","The short-period mode","The short-period mode is the quick, usually well-damped pitch oscillation — a second or two long — in which angle of attack and pitch rate swing together at almost constant speed. Its stiffness comes from C_mα and its da","Stability and Control",["physics:moment-of-inertia","math:second-order-linear"]],
  ["dutch-roll","Dutch roll and the spiral mode","Disturbed sideways, an aircraft shows three lateral motions: a quick roll subsidence, a slow spiral that may tighten or unwind, and the Dutch roll — a weaving yaw-and-roll oscillation. Dihedral effect and fin size pull t","Stability and Control"],
  ["fly-by-wire","Fly-by-wire and relaxed stability","In a fly-by-wire aircraft the pilot's controls send signals to computers, which move the surfaces to give the response asked for. This lets designers relax — or even reverse — the natural static stability, and lets the c","Stability and Control"],
  ["spins","Spins","A spin is a stalled, autorotating descent: one wing is more deeply stalled than the other, so the aircraft rolls and yaws continuously while it corkscrews down at low airspeed. The physics explains why spins start, why t","Stability and Control"],
  ["propulsion","Propulsion","How thrust is made by throwing air backwards: momentum theory, propulsive efficiency, propellers; turbojets, turbofans, turboprops, fuel consumption, ramjets and scramjets, rockets and electric propulsion.",""],
  ["thrust-basics","Thrust","How thrust is made, actuator-disc momentum theory, propulsive efficiency and propellers.","Propulsion"],
  ["thrust","How thrust is made","Every propulsor throws mass backwards and is pushed forwards by the reaction: thrust is the mass flow times the speed it adds, T = ṁ(Ve − V₀), plus a pressure term at the nozzle exit.","Propulsion",["physics:newtons-third-law","physics:conservation-of-momentum"]],
  ["momentum-theory","Actuator disc and momentum theory","Replace a propeller or rotor by a thin disc that adds a pressure jump: conservation of mass, momentum and energy then give the induced velocity, the ideal power T(V + v), and the power to hover √(T³/2ρA).","Propulsion",["physics:kinetic-energy"]],
  ["propulsive-efficiency","Propulsive efficiency","The useful power T·V₀ divided by the power put into the jet: ηp = 2/(1 + Ve/V₀). A jet only a little faster than the aircraft wastes little energy but needs a huge mass flow — the trade-off behind every engine.","Propulsion",["physics:kinetic-energy","physics:power","physics:efficiency"]],
  ["propellers","Propellers","A propeller is a set of rotating wings. Each blade section meets the air at the resultant of flight speed and rotation; blade angle minus inflow angle gives its angle of attack. Twist, pitch, tip speed, and fixed-pitch a","Propulsion"],
  ["propeller-efficiency","Advance ratio and propeller efficiency","Dimensional analysis reduces a propeller to the advance ratio J = V/(nD) and the coefficients C_T and C_P; efficiency is η = J·C_T/C_P. The propeller map shows η against J for every blade angle, and why constant-speed pr","Propulsion"],
  ["engines","Engines","Turbojets, turbofans, fuel consumption, turboprops, ramjets and scramjets, rockets and electric propulsion.","Propulsion"],
  ["turbojet","The turbojet","A gas turbine whose whole output is a fast jet: intake, compressor, combustor, turbine, nozzle. It runs the Brayton cycle, whose ideal efficiency 1 − r^(−(γ−1)/γ) rises with the pressure ratio; its thrust rises with turb","Propulsion",["physics:heat-engines","physics:thermodynamic-processes","physics:carnot-cycle"]],
  ["turbofan","The turbofan and bypass ratio","A turbojet core that also drives a large fan: most of the air bypasses the core and leaves as a slower, cooler jet. The bypass ratio sets the balance — higher ratios burn less fuel and make less noise, at the cost of siz","Propulsion"],
  ["tsfc","Specific fuel consumption","Thrust-specific fuel consumption is the fuel burned per unit thrust per hour: 0.5–0.6 lb/(lbf·h) for a modern turbofan in cruise, about 1 for a turbojet. It is linked to overall efficiency by η₀ = gV₀/(c_t Q_R) and to sp","Propulsion"],
  ["turboprop","Turboprops and turboshafts","A gas turbine whose turbine takes almost all the energy and drives a propeller through a gearbox (turboprop) or a rotor or shaft (turboshaft). Efficient and strong at low speed, limited by propeller tip speed to about 67","Propulsion",["physics:torque"]],
  ["ramjet-scramjet","Ramjets and scramjets","At high speed, slowing the air compresses it enough to make the compressor unnecessary: a ramjet is a duct with an intake, a combustor and a nozzle. It gives no static thrust. Above Mach 5–6 the air must burn while still","Propulsion"],
  ["rocket-propulsion","Rocket propulsion","A rocket carries its fuel and oxidiser, so it needs no air. Its economy is the specific impulse, I_sp = c/g₀; its speed gain is the rocket equation Δv = I_sp g₀ ln(m₀/m₁). Reaching orbit takes about 9.4 km/s — and stagin","Propulsion",["physics:rocket-propulsion","math:logarithms","physics:conservation-of-momentum"]],
  ["electric-propulsion","Electric aircraft propulsion","Electric motors are light, efficient and quiet, but batteries store about 50 times less energy per kilogram than kerosene and do not get lighter as they empty. Range = usable energy ÷ drag; distributed propulsion and air","Propulsion",["physics:electric-power"]],
  ["rotors-nature","Rotors, Wind and Nature","Helicopters and drones, the power to hover and autorotation; wind turbines and the Betz limit, sails; and how birds, insects and seeds fly.",""],
  ["rotorcraft","Rotorcraft and drones","The helicopter rotor, power to hover, autorotation, multicopters and forward flight.","Rotors, Wind and Nature"],
  ["helicopter-rotor","The helicopter rotor","A helicopter rotor is a set of long, thin wings turning at an almost constant speed. The pilot changes its thrust by changing the pitch of all the blades together (collective) and tilts the thrust by changing the pitch a","Rotors, Wind and Nature",["physics:torque","physics:angular-momentum","physics:centripetal-force"]],
  ["hover-power","Power to hover","A hovering rotor holds itself up by pushing air downward. Momentum theory gives the least power it can do it with, P = T^1.5 / √(2ρA): power grows faster than weight and falls as the rotor gets bigger — which is why heli","Rotors, Wind and Nature",["physics:power","physics:conservation-of-momentum"]],
  ["autorotation","Autorotation","If a helicopter's engine stops, the pilot lowers the collective and the rotor keeps turning by itself: air flowing up through the disc as the helicopter descends drives it like a windmill. The helicopter glides down stee","Rotors, Wind and Nature",["physics:rotational-kinetic-energy","physics:conservation-of-energy"]],
  ["multicopters","Multicopters and drones","A multicopter hovers on four, six or eight fixed-pitch propellers and steers by changing their speeds: more thrust on one side tilts it, and the difference in torque between the clockwise and anticlockwise propellers tur","Rotors, Wind and Nature",["physics:newtons-third-law","physics:torque","electronics:batteries"]],
  ["retreating-blade","Forward flight and retreating-blade stall","In forward flight the blade moving into the wind meets faster air than the blade moving away. To keep the rotor from rolling, the retreating blade must work at a higher angle of attack, and at high speed it stalls, while","Rotors, Wind and Nature",["physics:relative-velocity"]],
  ["wind-energy","Wind energy and sails","Wind turbines, the Betz limit, tip-speed ratio and sails.","Rotors, Wind and Nature"],
  ["wind-turbines","Wind turbines","A wind turbine is a propeller run backwards: it slows the wind and turns part of its kinetic energy into shaft power, P = ½ρAV³C_P. The cube of the wind speed rules everything — where turbines stand, how tall their tower","Rotors, Wind and Nature",["physics:power","physics:kinetic-energy"]],
  ["betz-limit","The Betz limit","No wind turbine can extract more than 16/27 — about 59 % — of the kinetic energy flowing through its disc. Stop the air completely and nothing flows through; leave it untouched and nothing is taken. The best compromise s","Rotors, Wind and Nature",["physics:conservation-of-momentum","physics:bernoullis-equation","math:derivative","math:optimization"]],
  ["tip-speed-ratio","Tip-speed ratio","The tip-speed ratio λ = ΩR/V compares how fast the blade tips move with the speed of the wind. Each rotor has a best value — about 7 to 9 for a modern three-bladed turbine, about 1 for an old farm windmill — and good con","Rotors, Wind and Nature",["physics:angular-kinematics","physics:torque"]],
  ["sails","Sails as wings","A sail is a soft wing stood on end. It works in the apparent wind — the true wind plus the headwind of the boat's own motion — and its lift, turned forward, drives the boat while the keel, a second wing in the water, sto","Rotors, Wind and Nature",["math:vector-addition","physics:relative-velocity"]],
  ["natural-flight","Flight in nature","Birds, insects, and seeds that glide and spin.","Rotors, Wind and Nature"],
  ["bird-flight","How birds fly","Birds are aircraft that flap: the downstroke makes lift and thrust together, the wing twisting and folding to do it. Each species' wings are a compromise between wing loading, aspect ratio and the way it lives — the alba","Rotors, Wind and Nature",["math:scaling-laws"]],
  ["insect-flight","Insect flight and unsteady lift","Insects fly at Reynolds numbers of about 10 to 10 000, sweeping their wings back and forth up to hundreds of times a second at high angles of attack. Steady-wing theory cannot hold them up; unsteady tricks do — a leading","Rotors, Wind and Nature",["physics:viscosity"]],
  ["seeds-gliders","Seeds that glide and spin","Plants spread their seeds on the wind by making them fall slowly. A maple seed autorotates like a tiny one-bladed helicopter rotor and falls at about 1 m/s; a dandelion floats under a parachute of bristles; the Javan cuc","Rotors, Wind and Nature"],
  ["aero-engineering","Testing, Computing and Structures","How aerodynamics is done: wind tunnels, scale models and similarity, flow visualisation, force and pressure measurement, flight testing; panel methods, CFD and turbulence models; wind loads on buildings, flutter and brid",""],
  ["wind-tunnels","Wind tunnels and experiments","Wind tunnels, scale models, flow visualisation, measuring forces and pressures, flight testing.","Testing, Computing and Structures"],
  ["wind-tunnel","Wind tunnels","A wind tunnel holds a model still and moves the air past it: a fan or a pressure difference drives the air through screens, a contraction and the test section, where forces, pressures and flow patterns are measured. Open","Testing, Computing and Structures"],
  ["similarity-testing","Scale models and similarity","A model predicts the full-size vehicle only if the flows are similar: the same shape, the same Mach number and the same Reynolds number. Usually both cannot be matched at once, so tunnels are pressurised or cooled, bound","Testing, Computing and Structures"],
  ["flow-visualisation","Flow visualisation","Air is invisible, so aerodynamicists make it show itself: smoke and tufts reveal streamlines and separation, oil films and china clay the flow at the surface, schlieren and shadowgraph the density changes of shocks, and ","Testing, Computing and Structures",["physics:refraction","physics:lasers"]],
  ["force-balance","Measuring forces and pressures","Wind-tunnel models are weighed by balances — external ones outside the test section, or strain-gauge balances inside the model — that measure all six force and moment components. Pressure taps and scanners map the pressu","Testing, Computing and Structures",["physics:stress-strain"]],
  ["flight-testing","Flight testing","In the end an aircraft is proven in the air. Flight tests calibrate the air data, measure performance, stability and handling, expand the envelope step by step towards the limits of speed, load, stall and flutter, and pr","Testing, Computing and Structures"],
  ["computation","Computational aerodynamics","Panel methods, CFD and turbulence models.","Testing, Computing and Structures"],
  ["panel-methods","Panel methods","Cover a body's surface with small flat panels, give each a source or vortex strength, and choose the strengths so that no air flows through any panel: a few hundred linear equations give the potential flow — and the pres","Testing, Computing and Structures",["math:systems-of-equations","math:gaussian-elimination"]],
  ["cfd","Computational fluid dynamics","CFD solves the equations of fluid flow numerically: the space round a body is divided into millions of small cells, mass, momentum and energy are balanced cell by cell, and the computer iterates until the flow settles. I","Testing, Computing and Structures",["math:pdes","math:numerical-integration","math:euler-method"]],
  ["turbulence-models","Turbulence models","Practical CFD cannot follow every turbulent eddy, so it solves for the average flow and models what the eddies do — usually as an extra \"eddy viscosity\". Mixing length, Spalart–Allmaras, k–ε and k–ω SST are the common ch","Testing, Computing and Structures",["physics:viscosity"]],
  ["wind-structures","Wind on structures","Wind loads on buildings, flutter and aeroelasticity, bridges and galloping.","Testing, Computing and Structures"],
  ["wind-loads","Wind loads on buildings","Wind pushes on a building's windward face and sucks on its sides, roof and back. Engineers take the dynamic pressure of the wind at each height, raise it for gusts, and multiply by pressure coefficients; codes such as th","Testing, Computing and Structures"],
  ["flutter","Flutter and aeroelasticity","Flutter is a self-excited vibration: above a critical speed, air forces that lag the bending and twisting of a flexible wing or control surface feed energy into the motion faster than the structure can dissipate it, and ","Testing, Computing and Structures",["physics:damped-oscillations","physics:driven-oscillations","math:damped-oscillator-ode"]],
  ["galloping-bridges","Bridges, galloping and Tacoma Narrows","Wind shakes slender structures in three ways: vortex shedding that locks on near one wind speed (bounded — a true resonance), galloping of shapes whose cross-wind force feeds their motion, and torsional flutter of bluff ","Testing, Computing and Structures",["physics:driven-oscillations","physics:damped-oscillations"]]
]);
